# Copy to `test/integration/.env`. Every value points the harness at a deployment; none of it
# configures a service, which is why none of it is in `config/env-manifest.yaml` (see the note at
# the top of `setup/jest.setup.cjs`).

# ── The gate ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing in this package runs without it. `pnpm run test` at the repository root will happily
# execute this package's suite; unset, every case reports as skipped with its reason, which is the
# intended state in CI.
SCVS_E2E=1

# ── Where the deployment is (plan prerequisite P1) ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `dev`'s real values, for reference — the placeholders below stay generic because this harness is
# meant to be pointed at any deployment, including a peer's:
#
#   SCVS_E2E_VERIFY_URL   https://dev.sv.example.com
#   SCVS_E2E_QUERY_URL    https://dev.sv.example.com       ← the SAME host
#   SCVS_E2E_RESULT_ROOT  https://results.dev.sv.example.com
#
# The two service URLs being one host is not a mistake: both public services sit behind ONE ALB,
# discriminated by path (`/api/verification/*` → verification-svc; `/api/query/*` → query-svc).
# `support/routes.ts` composes the paths;
# these variables carry only the origin. And the result root is a DIFFERENT HOST — the result tree is
# CloudFront over the dedicated `sv-dev-results` bucket, served at that host's root, which is also why
# it carries no `/public` segment any more. As of 2026-08-20 neither host resolves as written:
# `dev.sv.example.com` still A-aliases the CloudFront distribution and `results.dev.sv.example.com`
# does not exist, and creating it is an infrastructure change.
SCVS_E2E_VERIFY_URL="https://verify.example.io"
SCVS_E2E_QUERY_URL="https://query.example.io"
# The public console origin (not a page URL). It is usually the same ALB origin as the APIs, but is
# explicit so the browser case proves the deployed routing rather than assuming it.
SCVS_E2E_WEB_URL="https://verify.example.io"
# The static origin the published result tree is served from — the deployment's own
# SCVS_PUBLIC_RESULT_ROOT. `query-svc` does not proxy this tree, so result-tree cases read it here.
SCVS_E2E_RESULT_ROOT="https://results.example.io"
# P2: dev and staging must be entered into each other's query-service TVL. The E2E cases derive
# expected verifier identities directly from the public `/api/query/v1/verifiers` response.

# Default network for cases that do not fan out over both.
SCVS_E2E_NETWORK="testnet"

# ── Which classes of case this run may execute ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AUTO-INFRA changes deployment state (stops services, edits the TVL, injects storage faults) and
# needs a dedicated window plus a restoration plan.
# SCVS_E2E_ALLOW_INFRA=1
# PERF consumes shared build capacity and is run only through the explicit Locust commands.
# SCVS_E2E_ALLOW_PERF=1
# ── QRY-09: controlled peer outage (AUTO-INFRA) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This is an explicit, narrow AWS CLI control, not a generic command hook. The test reads the
# *running target environment's* public /api/query/v1/verifiers list to find `scvs-dev` and
# `scvs-staging`, then derives the peer in the opposite direction:
#
#   SCVS_E2E_ENV=dev      -> stops staging verification-svc
#   SCVS_E2E_ENV=staging  -> stops dev verification-svc
#
# Before any mutation it checks that SCVS_E2E_RESULT_ROOT's public verifier-manifest.json matches
# SCVS_E2E_ENV, then reads the peer ECS task definition and refuses to continue unless the service's
# SCVS_VERIFIER_ID matches that public peer identity. It records the current desired count and restores
# it in `finally`, including after an assertion failure. AWS credentials must be scoped to this one
# maintenance operation; an optional profile is named explicitly rather than inherited.
#
# SCVS_E2E_PEER_ECS_CLUSTER="<opposite-environment ECS cluster ARN or name>"
# SCVS_E2E_AWS_REGION="ap-southeast-2"
# SCVS_E2E_AWS_PROFILE="scvs-e2e-maintenance" # optional; omit for short-lived default credentials
# SCVS_E2E_PEER_OUTAGE_CONFIRM="stop-staging-verification-svc" # dev run
# SCVS_E2E_PEER_OUTAGE_CONFIRM="stop-dev-verification-svc"     # staging run
# SCVS_E2E_PEER_OUTAGE_TIMEOUT_MS=300000 # optional; default 5 minutes, minimum 30 seconds

# Turns every recorded TRD divergence back into a hard failure. Run this way to check whether a
# gap has closed; see `support/conformance.ts`.
# SCVS_E2E_STRICT_TRD=1

# ── Timing ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A cli-legacy build that has to compose its environment first is slow; 20 minutes is a starting
# point, not a measurement.
SCVS_E2E_JOB_TIMEOUT_MS=1200000
SCVS_E2E_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=3000
SCVS_E2E_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# ── Fixtures ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Defaults to the committed dev inventory, `fixtures/e2e-fixtures.dev.json`. CI points this at its
# ignored run-local inventory generated from fresh deployment evidence.
# SCVS_E2E_FIXTURES="fixtures/e2e-fixtures.dev.json"

# Required only when selecting E2E-SRC-05/cli_58_private_upload. This is the name of a funded local
# Stellar key already imported into the CLI, never a secret key or seed phrase.
# SCVS_FIXTURE_TESTNET_ACCOUNT="scvs-e2e-local"
# Explicitly selected timeout E2E cases deploy three run-scoped targets using this alias, so their
# Rule D retries cannot block another case or a later run on a stable target.

# E2E-UPG-01's selected test command also uses this signer to deploy an isolated v1 fixture and
# upgrade only that run-scoped address to v2. It does not change a shared contract or deployment.

# This verifier's result root — the identity every published entry carries as `verifier.url`, and
# what the suite matches its own entry on. Read from verifier-manifest.json's `url` when
# unset. Setting it **skips the manifest read entirely**, which is the point: use it for a deployment
# whose manifest the runner cannot reach, or whose published root differs from the host the runner
# reads results through.
# SCVS_E2E_VERIFIER_URL="https://results.dev.sv.example.com"

# This verifier's display name, as it appears in a published `verifier.name`. Self-claimed and
# optional: it is a last-resort match for a pre-cutover entry that carries no `url`,
# never the identity. Read from verifier-manifest.json when unset — and if SCVS_E2E_VERIFIER_URL is
# set and this is not, the suite simply holds no name and that fallback is skipped.
# SCVS_E2E_VERIFIER_NAME="SCVS Dev Verifier"

# A source archive that genuinely compiles to F-LEG-1's deployed bytes, for the private-upload
# case — a synthetic archive cannot reach `verified`.
# SCVS_E2E_PRIVATE_UPLOAD_PATH="/abs/path/to/f-leg-1-source.tar.gz"

# ── Which deployment artefact describes the target (📐 archive bounds) ────────────────────────
# `dev` or `staging` — the config/deploy/<env>/ directory for the deployment under test. Setting it
# is what lets E2E-SEC-03's two numeric rows run: the harness reads the three archive bounds from
# `config/deploy/<env>/builder.json` (SCVS_MAX_SOURCE_ARCHIVE_ENTRIES / _UNCOMPRESSED_BYTES /
# _BYTES), taking `value` before an explicitly numeric `default`.
#
# Not a hint — it is checked. A named environment whose artefact is missing, unparseable, or whose
# `target_env` disagrees is a *hard failure*, not a skip: that is a runner mistake, and degrading it
# to a skip would let a misconfigured run report the same green as a correct one.
#
# The artefact describes target state. Run `pnpm run generate:deploy-env` before the window and
# `eng-tools/diff-live-taskdefs.mjs --env <env>` where credentials allow, to check the live Batch
# job definition has not drifted from it.
#
# Leave it unset for a deployment this repository does not manage. The admissible source there is
# live-config evidence (the verifier's `policy_url` or an equivalent versioned/hashed proof); no such
# field exists yet, so the two numeric rows skip and say so.
# SCVS_E2E_ENV="dev"

# ── The staging observer (E2E-SRC-03 only) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SRC-03 asserts that a private upload's staging object is *gone* once the job terminates. Nothing on
# the public surface can see that, and the case says it must stay that way: there is no upload GET or
# HEAD route, the job body does not project `sourceRef`, and `GET …/log` refuses every key outside
# `build-logs/` — all three because that bucket holds other submitters' private tarballs. Do not add a
# public upload-read route to make this case easier.
#
# Re-submitting the `upload_id` and getting `400 upload_not_found` is NOT this. `deleteStagedUpload`
# deletes the DB row first and the object second, catching a failure on the second — so that probe
# passes in exactly the state the case is looking for. It is recorded as a diagnostic and never
# accepted as evidence.
#
# PREFERRED — an existence-only control-plane probe. It holds the S3 permission itself and answers
# `{"exists": boolean, "size"?: number}` for one exact key given as `?key=`. This is the only
# admissible option against a bucket shared with real submissions, because AWS has no HEAD-only
# permission: `HeadObject` requires `s3:GetObject`, i.e. the ability to read every private tarball in
# the bucket.
# SCVS_E2E_STAGING_PROBE_URL="https://e2e-control.example.io/staging-exists"
#
# FALLBACK — direct HeadObject, admissible only against an ISOLATED E2E staging bucket reached with
# short-lived credentials. Both variables are required; the region is never inferred, because a
# default region would HEAD a same-named bucket elsewhere and report `absent` for an object that
# exists. Naming the bound deployment's own SCVS_STORAGE_BUCKET here is refused at load rather than
# warned about — see support/env.ts. Note also that a HEAD 404 on a versioned bucket only proves the
# current version is unreadable; historical versions and backups are SEC-15's subject, not SRC-03's.
# SCVS_E2E_STAGING_BUCKET="scvs-e2e-staging-isolated"
# SCVS_E2E_STAGING_REGION="ap-southeast-2"
#
# The approved window between the job's first terminal observation and the object disappearing. An
# operational criterion for the environment, not a value the suite may pick: the terminal status row
# is persisted *before* the best-effort delete, so the first post-terminal HEAD is expected to race.
# It is checked against the upload's own `expires_at` at run time and must sit far inside it —
# otherwise the expiry sweep, not job termination, could be what satisfied the assertion.
# SCVS_E2E_STAGING_DELETION_DEADLINE_MS=60000

# E2E-SRC-03/on_internal_error: a controlled, structurally valid contract id that is deliberately
# absent from SCVS_E2E_NETWORK for the maintenance window. The real chain reader fails its fetch and
# the public job response carries `build_failed` with `error=stellar_contract_fetch_failed`; the test
# then observes cleanup of the valid private F-PRIV upload. Do not use a malformed id or a live target.
# SCVS_E2E_INTERNAL_ERROR_CONTRACT_ID="C..."

# ── The nine deployment parameters (📐 cases) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All nine are specification gaps: nothing defines them,
# and most should be published in `policy.json`. A 📐 case whose parameter is absent skips naming
# it, because the case says explicitly not to invent a value.
#
# ★ The three archive bounds are NOT among these any more, and setting them here is now an error
# rather than an override: `SCVS_E2E_LIMIT_TAR_ENTRIES`, `SCVS_E2E_LIMIT_UNCOMPRESSED_BYTES` and
# `SCVS_E2E_LIMIT_ARCHIVE_BYTES` are refused at load with a message naming the two admissible
# sources. A harness environment variable is not evidence about a deployment — it is the harness
# asserting its own guess and the run reporting the result as conformance, which is the one outcome
# the coverage analysis rules out. Use SCVS_E2E_ENV.
# ★ SCVS_E2E_QUOTA_SUBMIT is what E2E-SEC-11 and E2E-SEC-12 size their bursts from, and both
# send `quota + 20` requests per leg — so read it from the target's own deploy contract
# (`config/deploy/<env>/verification-svc.json`, key SCVS_SUBMIT_ABUSE_MAX_PER_WINDOW) rather
# than guessing; CI exports it that way. dev's is deliberately large while the load-testing
# overrides stand (1500/min since 2026-08-26), which is a real burst to point at a deployment.
# SCVS_E2E_QUOTA_SUBMIT=""
# SCVS_E2E_WINDOW_SUBMIT_MS=""
# SCVS_E2E_QUOTA_QUERY=""
# SCVS_E2E_WINDOW_QUERY_MS=""
# SCVS_E2E_LIMIT_WALLCLOCK_MS=""
# SCVS_E2E_LIMIT_SSE_CONNECTIONS=""
# SCVS_E2E_PERF_DRAIN_TIMEOUT_MS="14400000"
# PERF-01 is a Locust closed-loop load test: each user repeats submit -> terminal verified -> report
# validation against one exclusive target address. The iteration cap is a mandatory real-build cost guard.
# SCVS_E2E_PERF_USERS="10"
# SCVS_E2E_PERF_DURATION_SECONDS="3600"
# SCVS_E2E_PERF_THINK_TIME_SECONDS="60"
# SCVS_E2E_PERF_MAX_REBUILDS="500"
# SCVS_E2E_PERF_OUTPUT_DIR="artifacts/e2e-reports/perf-01"
# PERF-02 is a fixed-interval burst Locust workload. It launches BURST_SIZE concurrent flows at every
# interval regardless of whether older flows have settled, up to MAX_IN_FLIGHT and the rebuild cost cap.
# SCVS_E2E_PERF_OPEN_DURATION_SECONDS="600"
# SCVS_E2E_PERF_OPEN_BURST_SIZE="20"
# SCVS_E2E_PERF_OPEN_BURST_INTERVAL_SECONDS="5"
# SCVS_E2E_PERF_OPEN_MAX_IN_FLIGHT="20"

# ── P8 security-probe inputs (E2E-SEC-09 / E2E-SEC-10, AUTO-INFRA) ────────────────────────────
# These paths are local to the controlled runner and must not be committed or uploaded as ordinary
# test artifacts. SEC-09's exporter observes one diagnostic Batch job and writes a denial summary;
# set the job id separately so the test rejects stale evidence. SEC-10's capture contains signed
# URLs after both builder uploads complete, plus one separately minted disposable control URL.
# The exact JSON contracts and Operations export requirements are in test/integration/README.md.
# SCVS_E2E_SEC09_EVIDENCE_PATH="artifacts/e2e-sec09-evidence.json"
# SCVS_E2E_SEC09_JOB_ID="<AWS Batch job id observed by the P8 exporter>"
# SCVS_E2E_SEC10_CAPTURE_PATH="artifacts/e2e-sec10-capture.json"
# SCVS_E2E_UPLOAD_URL_TTL_MS=900000

# ── SUB-09/11/12 Operations evidence (AUTO-INFRA) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# These files bind a public job id to deployment state that the app API must not expose: a temporary
# infrastructure fault, a policy-changing joint redeploy, or a paused-dispatch contract upgrade.
# They are local, restricted artifacts. Exact versioned JSON contracts and window ordering are in
# test/integration/README.md; never put credentials, signed URLs or secret values in them.
# SCVS_E2E_SUB09_INTERNAL_ERROR_EVIDENCE_PATH="artifacts/e2e-sub09-internal-error.json"
# SCVS_E2E_SUB11_EVIDENCE_PATH="artifacts/e2e-sub11-policy-recheck.json"
# SCVS_E2E_SUB12_EVIDENCE_PATH="artifacts/e2e-sub12-drift.json"
# E2E-DEDUP-06/internal_error_requeued: an operator applies a temporary approved fault to a
# valid job, records its first failed state, restores the fault, then immediately runs the case.
# SCVS_E2E_DEDUP06_INTERNAL_ERROR_EVIDENCE_PATH="artifacts/e2e-dedup06-internal-error.json"

# ── SRC-08 / SRC-09: a public source URL that can be switched off ─────────────────────────────
# §6.2 rule 1 is "a contract verified in January is still verified in June when the repository is
# deleted", and it cannot be tested against a URL nobody can take away. `dark-source-server.mjs`
# serves F-DARK's archive and exposes POST /control {"available": boolean}; point this at that route.
#
# Two things the case checks and will refuse to run without, because either one produces a green run
# that proves nothing:
#   1. F-DARK's `source_uri` in the fixture inventory must be an address the DEPLOYMENT resolves —
#      the verifier fetches the source, not the harness, so the 127.0.0.1 default is local smoke only.
#   2. F-DARK's archive must not be one another case already retained. The default is the canonical
#      archive, whose hash is F-LEG-1's, and SRC-01 stores that — so /visibility_reflects_reality
#      would pass by finding SRC-01's copy. Use an archive with its own hash (the bulk corpus has 200)
#      via SCVS_FIXTURE_DARK_ARCHIVE and record that hash in registry.yaml and the inventory.
# SCVS_E2E_DARK_SOURCE_CONTROL_URL="https://e2e-fixtures.example.io/dark-source/control"

# ── SRC-11: prerequisite P12, object-store faults scoped to ONE prefix ────────────────────────
# POST {url} {"prefix": "sources/", "mode": "fail_writes" | "healthy"}. Writes under the named prefix
# fail; reads stay healthy, and `wasms/`/`reports/` stay healthy in both modes.
#
# ★ The scoping is the precondition, not a detail. Break `wasms/` or `reports/` as well and the run is
# E2E-PUB-16 (publication fault recovery) wearing SRC-11's name, and its "publication was unaffected"
# assertion would be measuring the injector's own blast radius. Reads must stay healthy too, or HA3
# cannot tell "the object is not there" from "the object cannot be read".
# SCVS_E2E_OBJECT_STORE_FAULT_URL="https://e2e-control.example.io/object-store-fault"

# ── Refused outright, not merely ignored ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SCVS_E2E_ASSUME_SOURCE_DARK and SCVS_E2E_ASSUME_RETENTION_FAILED throw at load. SRC-08 and SRC-11
# are about states somebody has to actually establish — a source that really 404s, a prefix whose
# writes really fail. Asserting the premise from an environment variable makes the case report a
# green result for a condition that never existed. Same reasoning as the SCVS_E2E_LIMIT_* refusal.
